{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"chatcmpl-b4ece4c0-05c3-4ca0-9537-261aafa556da","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"chatcmpl-tool-a829393ecb725b07","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/private/var/folders/h5/l8qslbp55w5621qf9ngsh5jc0000gn/T/epic-RHAISTRAT-8009-ito67jrk/artifacts/epic-tasks/RHAISTRAT-8009-decomposition.md","content":"# Decomposition Summary: RHAISTRAT-8009\n\nWorkspace-aware artifact serving for multi-tenant MLflow deployments.\n\n## Triage\n\nNot triaged. The strategy is M-L (4-7 sprints), spans three affected components\n(MLflow, MLflow Operator, mlflow-kubernetes-plugins) with both server-side and\noperator-topology work streams, and contains open questions and\nvalidation-flagged assumptions that gate downstream scope. Full decomposition\nfollows.\n\n## Staff Engineer Input Overrides\n\nThe Staff Engineer / SME Input section is non-empty and takes precedence. Three\noverrides were applied; the strategy body already reflects all three, and this\ndecomposition follows the corrected strategy:\n\n1. **No new flag — combine existing flags.** The AI-generated technical\n   approach's proposed `--artifacts-only-workspace-aware` flag and\n   `spec.artifactsOnly.workspaceAware` CR field are removed. All epics scope\n   the capability as composition of the existing `--artifacts-only` and\n   `--enable-workspaces` flags with a required explicit `--workspace-store-uri`.\n2. **Operator work is a split deployment, not a config toggle.** The operator\n   epic scopes a dedicated artifacts-only Deployment with its own Service and\n   HTTPRoute plus artifact traffic routing from the main tracking deployment —\n   not a single CR field / Helm values propagation.\n3. **SSAR detail is over-specified.** Workspace authorization reuses the\n   existing auth plugin infrastructure without prescribing Kubernetes API\n   mechanics (no SelfSubjectAccessReview pseudo-resource dictation at epic\n   level). kube-rbac-proxy sidecar and `--app-name kubernetes-auth` are\n   unchanged.\n\n## Component Mapping\n\nAll epics use the canonical Jira component `MLflow` (the only matching entry in\n`.context/rhai-components.txt`). The strategy's \"MLflow Operator\" and\n\"mlflow-kubernetes-plugins\" components have no separate canonical entries:\n`MLflow` is the single most-relevant canonical name for each epic and the\ncomponent span is noted per epic below. `submit.py` would silently strip\nnon-canonical values, so no epic uses \"MLflow Operator\" or\n\"mlflow-kubernetes-plugins\" verbatim.\n\n## Epic List\n\n| ID | Title | Type | Team | Priority |\n|----|-------|------|------|----------|\n| RHAISTRAT-8009-E001 | Workspace resolution feasibility in the artifacts-only server context | Investigation | MLflow Team | P0 |\n| RHAISTRAT-8009-E002 | Artifact traffic routing design for the split-server topology | Investigation | MLflow Team | P1 |\n| RHAISTRAT-8009-E003 | Workspace-aware artifacts-only serving in the MLflow tracking server | Implementation | MLflow Team | P0 |\n| RHAISTRAT-8009-E004 | mlflow-kubernetes-plugins workspace resolution in the artifacts-only context | Implementation | MLflow Team | P0 |\n| RHAISTRAT-8009-E005 | MLflow operator split-server topology for the artifacts-only instance | Implementation | MLflow Team | P1 |\n| RHAISTRAT-8009-E006 | End-to-end integration validation of workspace-aware artifact serving | Implementation | MLflow Team | P0 |\n\nPriority notes: E004 carries a P1 HLR (HLR-7) but blocks E003, which delivers\nall P0 HLRs, so it inherits P0 by priority inheritance. E006 is P0 because it\nis the acceptance gate for the P0 HLRs' end-to-end criteria. E002 and E005 gate\nonly P1 HLRs (HLR-5, HLR-6) and stay P1.\n\n## Dependency DAG\n\n```mermaid\ngraph TD\n    E001[RHAISTRAT-8009-E001 Workspace resolution feasibility] --> E004[RHAISTRAT-8009-E004 Plugin workspace resolution]\n    E001 --> E003[RHAISTRAT-8009-E003 Server workspace-aware artifacts-only]\n    E004 --> E003\n    E002[RHAISTRAT-8009-E002 Routing design] --> E005[RHAISTRAT-8009-E005 Operator split-server topology]\n    E003 --> E006[RHAISTRAT-8009-E006 End-to-end integration validation]\n    E005 --> E006\n```\n\n## DAG Justification\n\n| Edge | Rule | Rationale |\n|------|------|-----------|\n| E001 → E004 | Rule 3 (Investigation gates downstream) | The feasibility investigation determines whether the plugin's workspace resolution is coupled to tracking store initialization; if coupled, E004's scope is a refactor (rewrite), not just verification. True gate: `rewrite`. |\n| E001 → E003 | Rule 3 (Investigation gates downstream) | The same investigation determines whether artifact request paths carry enough information to resolve workspace membership — the premise of E003's workspace validation. True gate: `rewrite`. |\n| E004 → E003 | Rule 12 (library → consumer serial) | The server's artifact-request workspace validation builds against the plugin's workspace resolution interface (the `mlflow.workspace_provider` entry point loaded via `WorkspaceStoreRegistry`). The consumer waits for the library surface it calls. |\n| E002 → E005 | Rule 3 (Investigation gates downstream) | The routing design determines the operator's HTTPRoute design and whether the tracking server needs a delegation configuration change; it rewrites E005's routing scope. True gate: `rewrite`. |\n| E003 → E006 | Rule 13 (artifact consumer serial) | End-to-end validation exercises the workspace-aware artifacts-only server behavior that E003 produces; the test suite builds against that implemented capability. |\n| E005 → E006 | Rule 13 (artifact consumer serial) | End-to-end validation exercises the split-server topology (dedicated Deployment, Service, HTTPRoute, artifact traffic routing) that the operator work produces. |\n\nParallelism (no edges, by design):\n\n- E001 ∥ E002 — independent unknowns (server/plugin resolution vs. operator\n  routing design); different questions, different codebases.\n- E005 ∥ E003 / E004 — different repositories with no shared build artifact\n  (Rule 14). The operator's rendered-resource acceptance criteria are\n  verifiable without the new server capability; the coupling between operator\n  topology and server behavior is expressed as E006's end-to-end validation,\n  never as a DAG edge.\n\n### Investigation outcome analysis (bounded outcomes, no branch epics)\n\nBoth investigations have bounded outcomes (≤2 each), but in every outcome the\nsame main-plan epics exist — only their scope changes. Per the branch-epic\ncontract (branch files are for epics whose *existence* depends on an outcome),\nthe outcomes are recorded as `gate_failure_impact` on the gated epics instead\nof conditional branch files:\n\n- **E001 outcome A** (resolution feasible, plugin not coupled): E004 reduces to\n  verification plus tests. **Outcome B** (coupled): E004 additionally factors\n  workspace resolution into an independent initialization path — `rewrite`.\n- **E002 outcome A** (Gateway-level path routing suffices): E005 implements\n  routing purely via HTTPRoute resources. **Outcome B** (tracking server needs\n  a delegation configuration change): the server-side delegation config is\n  added to the server workstream's scope and the operator's routing scope is\n  rewritten accordingly — `rewrite`.\n\n## HLR Traceability Matrix\n\n| HLR | Priority | Epic(s) |\n|-----|----------|---------|\n| HLR-1: `--artifacts-only` composable with `--enable-workspaces` + `--workspace-store-uri` | P0 | E003 |\n| HLR-2: artifact requests validated against workspace membership; non-members denied HTTP 403 | P0 | E003 (with E004 providing the resolution it calls) |\n| HLR-3: fail-closed when workspace store unreachable | P0 | E003 |\n| HLR-4: single-tenant / no-workspace-flag deployments retain current behavior | P0 | E003, E005 (operator topology is opt-in), E006 (regression suite) |\n| HLR-5: operator deploys dedicated artifacts-only Deployment + Service + HTTPRoute | P1 | E005 |\n| HLR-6: main tracking deployment routes artifact traffic to the dedicated instance | P1 | E005 |\n| HLR-7: auth plugin workspace resolution works without tracking store initialization | P1 | E004 |\n| HLR-8: audit logging for workspace access denials via server logs | P2 | E003 (bundled) — emitted from the same artifact-request denial code path as HLR-2/HLR-3; a separate epic would instrument the same handlers twice, and the strategy's own HLR text pins the channel to server logs, so it is not separately schedulable |\n\nAll HLRs are covered; no orphaned requirements. HLR-8 is the only\nlower-priority HLR kept bundled, per the inline rationale above.\n\n## Health Warnings\n\n- **E005 exceeds the ~2-week epic guideline (managed).** E005 is 2-3 sprints\n  by the strategy's effort arithmetic. Kept whole because the split-server\n  topology is one logical operator change — a single reconciliation path\n  rendering one coordinated set of Helm resources (Deployment, Service,\n  HTTPRoute, routing config) — and splitting it would fragment the chart\n  templates and reconciliation logic across artificial boundaries. Re-split\n  trigger: the routing design investigation lands on an approach requiring\n  substantive server-side delegation changes in addition to the operator\n  topology, at which point the epic splits into topology-provisioning and\n  traffic-routing epics along that boundary.\n- **No latency target for per-request workspace validation (open question,\n  PM/Engineering).** The strategy's Performance NFR sets no number, and the\n  open question of whether short-TTL caching of workspace membership decisions\n  is required at launch is unresolved. This does not gate any epic's\n  existence or approach (validation is implemented the same way either way);\n  it is recorded as a risk on E003 with caching as a deferrable follow-up.\n  Human follow-up: PM/Engineering to set the acceptable overhead.\n- **Audit telemetry channel (open question, PM/Security).** HLR-8 commits to\n  server logs; whether denials should additionally emit to OTLP or the\n  Kubernetes audit log is unresolved and out of the committed scope. Human\n  follow-up: PM/Security decision; expansion would be a follow-up strategy.\n- **Strategy-level process gates pending.** Architecture Review, ODH/RHOAI\n  Build Onboarding, Licence Validation, and other prerequisites are all\n  \"Pending human review\" at the strategy level. No epic models these as\n  blocking work; if any gate fails review, affected epic scope may need\n  revision.\n- **Initial implementation scoped to the S3 artifact store.** Per the\n  strategy's risk mitigation, artifact path resolution is scoped to the\n  standard RHOAI S3 configuration (`{experiment_id}/{run_id}/artifacts/`);\n  non-standard artifact roots are documented as a limitation (folded into\n  E003 scope), not supported.\n\n## Tiered Delivery\n\nNot applicable. All dependencies are internal and exist today (workspace\nstore, auth plugin, Gateway API, kube-rbac-proxy); there is no external\ndependency with uncertain timing, so no Tier 1 / Tier 2 split is needed.\nPartial value ordering still exists naturally: the server-side capability\n(P0) is deliverable independently of the operator topology (P1), since\nworkspace-aware artifacts-only serving works for manually deployed instances\nbefore the operator automates the split topology.\n"}}],"model":"rits/zai-org/glm-5-3","stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":51392,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-a01dccb897beabeb","agent_id":"afb03082dd7d72f20","session_id":"994175ee-dc03-4507-860b-d2f574f96031","uuid":"47fee742-486a-460d-a302-22be42aabc10","timestamp":"2026-10-26T20:47:39.567Z","subagent_type":"claude","task_description":"Decompose RHAISTRAT-8009"}
