{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"chatcmpl-90f4a020-da51-4a2b-abba-b69f3b3e8286","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"chatcmpl-tool-81bc514051e55ccc","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/private/var/folders/h5/l8qslbp55w5621qf9ngsh5jc0000gn/T/epic-RHAISTRAT-8009-ito67jrk/artifacts/epic-tasks/RHAISTRAT-8009-E005.md","content":"# MLflow operator split-server topology for the artifacts-only instance\n\n## Description\n\nExtend the MLflow operator to deploy a dedicated artifacts-only MLflow\ninstance as a separate Deployment with its own Service and HTTPRoute, forming\na split-server topology: the main tracking deployment handles experiment\ntracking, model registry, and prompt registry, while the artifacts-only\ninstance handles artifact serving with workspace validation. The operator\nadditionally configures the main tracking deployment to route artifact\ntraffic to the dedicated instance, per the routing design. This is a\nsplit-server topology, not a single-deployment configuration toggle.\n\n## Scope\n\n- New Helm chart templates for the artifacts-only Deployment, Service, and\n  HTTPRoute, rendered through the operator's existing Helm-in-operator\n  pattern (MLflow CR spec converted to Helm values, rendered through\n  embedded chart templates, applied via Server-Side Apply).\n- Propagate workspace configuration (`--enable-workspaces`,\n  `--workspace-store-uri`, `--app-name kubernetes-auth`) to the artifacts-only\n  instance using the same configuration mechanisms already used for the main\n  tracking deployment, together with `--artifacts-only` and\n  `--serve-artifacts`.\n- Configure the main tracking deployment to route artifact traffic to the\n  dedicated artifacts-only instance, per the routing design (HTTPRoute-level\n  routing and/or tracking server delegation configuration, as the design\n  investigation determined).\n- Create Gateway API HTTPRoute resources for the artifacts-only instance\n  conditionally based on runtime CRD discovery, consistent with the\n  operator's existing pattern for optional dependencies.\n- Deploy the kube-rbac-proxy sidecar on the artifacts-only instance\n  identically to the main tracking deployment (authentication layer\n  unchanged).\n- Operator validation: reject MLflow CR configurations where the\n  artifacts-only instance is enabled but lacks the workspace flags, so that\n  misconfiguration cannot result in artifact requests bypassing workspace\n  validation.\n- Backwards compatibility: MLflow CRs that do not enable split-server\n  artifact serving reconcile to the current single-deployment topology\n  unchanged.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- Given an MLflow CR with split-server artifact serving enabled, when the\n  operator reconciles the CR, then a dedicated artifacts-only Deployment,\n  Service, and HTTPRoute are created separate from the main tracking\n  Deployment — measured by the presence of distinct rendered resources with\n  the correct flag combinations.\n- The artifacts-only Deployment's container command runs with\n  `--artifacts-only --enable-workspaces --workspace-store-uri kubernetes://\n  --app-name kubernetes-auth --serve-artifacts` — measured by inspecting the\n  container command in the rendered Deployment spec.\n- The main tracking deployment is configured to route artifact traffic to\n  the dedicated artifacts-only instance per the adopted routing design —\n  measured by the rendered routing configuration and HTTPRoute resources.\n- HTTPRoute resources for the artifacts-only instance are created only when\n  the Gateway API CRDs are present at runtime — measured by reconcile\n  behavior with and without the CRDs (conditional discovery pattern).\n- An MLflow CR enabling the artifacts-only instance without workspace flags\n  is rejected with a clear validation error — measured by reconcile status /\n  event output.\n- MLflow CRs without split-server artifact serving reconcile to the current\n  topology unchanged — measured by rendered-resource parity with the\n  pre-change output (rollback / opt-in safety).\n\n## HLR Traceability\n\n- HLR-5 (P1): operator deploys a dedicated artifacts-only instance as a\n  separate Deployment with its own Service and HTTPRoute.\n- HLR-6 (P1): main tracking deployment routes artifact traffic to the\n  dedicated artifacts-only instance.\n- HLR-4 (P0, backwards-compatibility aspect): the split topology is opt-in;\n  CRs without it retain current behavior.\n\n## Risks / Notes\n\n- Split-deployment operational complexity: two Deployments, two Services,\n  two HTTPRoutes, and artifact traffic routing add operational surface;\n  misconfiguration could let artifact requests bypass workspace validation.\n  Mitigation in scope: declarative single-CR management plus reconcile-time\n  validation rejecting artifacts-only configurations that lack workspace\n  flags.\n- The rendered-resource acceptance criteria are verifiable without the new\n  server capability; end-to-end behavior of the deployed topology is\n  validated by the integration validation workstream.\n- This epic exceeds the ~2-week guideline (2-3 sprints by the strategy's\n  effort arithmetic). Kept whole because the split topology is one logical\n  operator change — a single reconciliation path rendering one coordinated\n  set of resources. Re-split trigger: the routing design lands on an\n  approach requiring substantive server-side delegation changes in addition\n  to the operator topology, at which point split into topology-provisioning\n  and traffic-routing epics.\n"}}],"model":"rits/zai-org/glm-5-3","stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":58324,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-a01dccb897beabeb","agent_id":"afb03082dd7d72f20","session_id":"994175ee-dc03-4507-860b-d2f574f96031","uuid":"c60cacaa-40d0-4f6d-9f24-a7ba6df4c28c","timestamp":"2026-10-26T20:48:44.049Z","subagent_type":"claude","task_description":"Decompose RHAISTRAT-8009"}
