g design requires a tracking-server-side configuration change to delegate artifact operations... implement that configuration change.\" This is gated by E002's outcome, but E003 has no dependency on E002. Hmm. E003's scope includes conditional work that depends on E002's routing design outcome. Is this a missing edge? The decomposition summary's investigation outcome analysis says for E002 outcome B: \"the server-side delegation config is added to the server workstream's scope\" — i.e., folded into E003's scope. But E003 doesn't depend on E002 in the DAG. E003 could start and finish before E002's design lands, then the delegation config would be unimplemented.\n\nIs this a missing DAG edge? E003's scope explicitly includes \"If the split-server routing design requires a tracking-server-side configuration change... implement that configuration change.\" That's a conditional data dependency: E003 consumes E002's design outcome. Per criterion 2 cross-check completeness: \"scan epic content (scope, ACs, descriptions) for data/artifact dependencies not captured in the DAG — e.g., an epic that consumes a schema, image, or API produced by another epic but has no edge to it. A missing edge discoverable from epic content is a critical issue even if the diagram and frontmatter are consistent.\"\n\nHmm. E003 consumes E002's routing design outcome (conditionally). There's no edge E002 → E003. Is this a genuine missing edge? Alternatively, the design could be handled by E005 folding the change... no, E005 says \"the server-side delegation change lands in the server workstream\". So E003's scope has a conditional item depending on E002's outcome, with no DAG edge.\n\nCounter-argument: E002 outcome B also rewrites E005, and E005's fallback says \"the server-side delegation change lands in the server workstream\". If E002 lands after E003 completes, the delegation config would be new work in the server workstream — possibly a scope addition rather than a dependency. But E003's scope as written includes it, so the epic as scoped consumes E002's output. This is a discoverable data dependency not in the DAG. Severity critical per the instructions? \"A missing edge discoverable from epic content is a critical issue.\"\n\nHmm, but wait — is it truly a dependency, or is the conditional scope item just awkward? The instructions are pretty explicit. Let me think about whether this is a deliberate design: the decomposition summary's \"Investigation outcome analysis\" for E002 outcome B says \"the server-side delegation config is added to the server workstream's scope and the operator's routing scope is rewritten accordingly — rewrite.\" The \"rewrite\" applies to E005 (gate_failure_impact). E003 is not gated by E002, has no gate_failure_impact mentioning E002.\n\nOptions for flagging: I could flag it as a critical issue under DAG Coherence (missing edge where a data/artifact dependency exists). That would zero criterion 2 and force revise. Or is it arguably minor?\n\nLet me think harder. The dependency: E003's scope item is conditional (\"If the split-server routing design requires...\"). E003's core P0 work (flag composition, validation, fail-closed) does not depend on E002. Only an optional, conditional add-on does. The DAG rules: investigation edges 4-5 — an epic whose scope is gated by an investigation should depend on it. Here part of E003's scope is gated by E002's outcome.\n\nActually, there's a subtlety: the decomposition deliberately kept E002 → E005 only, and describes the outcome-B path as \"the server-side delegation config is added to the server workstream's scope\" — i.e., if outcome B occurs, E003's scope is amended. But E003 has no gated_by E002 and no dependency. If E002 runs in parallel with E003 (both start at the beginning — E003 blocked only by E001/E004), E003 could complete before E002's design decision. Then the delegation config work has no home at execution time (E003 done, E005 says it lands in the server workstream). This i