pted design (and the server-side delegation change lands in the server workstream); ...\"\n\nSo the plan is: E002 outcome B → E005 rewritten, server-side delegation change lands in \"the server workstream\" = E003. But E003 has no relationship to E002. If E003 is complete by then (likely, since E001→E004→E003 is the P0 critical path and E002∥E001), the delegation change has no epic. The decomposition has silently assumed it can be absorbed. There's no gate_failure_impact on E003 for E002, no dependency, no branch.\n\nYes, I'll flag as a missing-edge issue. Severity: I keep going back and forth between critical and major. Let me settle with the instruction text, which is unambiguous about the failure mode it wants caught: \"A missing edge discoverable from epic content is a critical issue even if the diagram and frontmatter are consistent with each other.\" The scope bullet is epic content; the dependency (on E002's design artifact) is real; the edge is absent. Critical. Criterion 2 → 0. pass: false, recommendation: revise.\n\nHmm, wait. Let me also consider whether the \"correction\" framing matters — as reviewer I describe the issue; the revise agent fixes.\n\nActually, hold on. Let me reconsider whether the dependency direction could be considered acceptable because the conditional bullet says \"If ... requires\" — meaning at the time E003 executes, the routing design may or may not have landed. If it hasn't landed, E003 can't know whether to implement the config change. So either E003 waits for E002 (missing edge) or E003 completes and the work is orphaned. Either way, defect. Confirmed.\n\nAre there other issues to check?\n\nLet me check the canonical components file and architecture context existence.\n\nAlso check E002's cluster_hardware_dependence: -1 vs E001's 0. What's the rubric scale? Let me look at the skill's signal rubric to verify -1 is a valid value. If the rubric is 0/1 booleans, -1 would be invalid. Let me check skills/epic-decompose for the rubric