E004 parallelism is correctly\nkept edge-free (Rule 14, different repos, no shared build artifact); no\nrepo/konflux/license onboarding epics exist, so Rules 7/8/10 parallelism is\nnot implicated.\n\nHowever, there is a **critical missing edge discoverable from epic\ncontent**: E003's scope contains the bullet \"If the split-server routing\ndesign requires a tracking-server-side configuration change to delegate\nartifact operations to a dedicated artifacts-only instance, implement that\nconfiguration change.\" That bullet consumes E002's routing-design outcome —\nE002 is the epic that determines whether such a server-side change is\nrequired. The decomposition summary's investigation-outcome analysis and\nE005's `gate_failure_impact` both confirm that under E002 outcome B \"the\nserver-side delegation config is added to the server workstream's scope\"\n(i.e., E003's scope). Yet E003 has no dependency on E002, no `gated_by`\nE002, and no `gate_failure_impact` covering that outcome. Rule 3 mandates a\nblocking edge from an Investigation to ALL affected Implementations; E003 is\nan affected Implementation by the decomposition's own account. As built,\nE003 (blocked only by E001/E004) can start and complete while E002 (which\nruns parallel to E001) is still open, leaving the conditional delegation\nwork unowned in the plan if outcome B materializes. The diagram and\nfrontmatter are consistent with each other, but both omit an edge the epic\ncontent requires — per the review contract this is a critical issue and\nzeroes the criterion. Compliant fixes include: adding an E002 → E003 edge\nwith `gated_by`/`gate_failure_impact` recorded on E003 (note this would make\nE002 block P0 work, so E002's priority inheritance must be re-derived), or\nremoving the conditional bullet from E003's unconditional scope and\nmodelling the outcome-B server-side change so it is owned and gated\nexplicitly (e.g., via E005's rewrite or a recorded gate), rather than as an\nungated scope bullet in an epic with no relation to E002.\n\n### 3. Epic Boundaries (2/2)\nAll epics are MLflow Team with canonical component `MLflow` (the only\nmatching entry in `.context/rhai-components.txt`; the summary documents that\n\"MLflow Operator\" and \"mlflow-kubernetes-plugins\" have no canonical entries\nand would be stripped by submit.py). The E003/E004/E005 split follows\nrepo/sub-system seams (server, plugin, operator), not the HLR list. E005's\nsize over the ~2-week guideline is a properly recorded watch item: the\nHealth Warnings entry records the estimate (2-3 sprints), the why-not-split\nrationale (one logical operator change, single reconciliation path), and a\nconcrete conditional re-split trigger — shape (b), not docked and no split\ndemanded. No scope duplication: E006 validates the surfaces E003/E005\nproduce rather than rebuilding them. No work-product type mixing: the\n\"document limitations\" item in E003 is an incidental documentation touch-up\nexempt from the content-authoring split.\n\n### 4. Type Correctness (2/2)\nBoth Investigations genuinely change downstream scope/existence: E001's\nanswers determine whether E004 is verification or refactor and whether\nE003's path-to-workspace resolution premise holds; E002's design determines\nE005's routing scope. Every `gated_by` target appears in the epic's direct\n`dependencies` (E003→E001, E004→E001, E005→E002), and every `gate_failure_impact`\nhas both `action` and `fallback_approach` populated with substantive\nrewrites. E006 is correctly ungated (it depends only on Implementations).\nThe ungated E002→E003 relationship is scored under DAG Coherence above.\n\n### 5. AI Implementability Scoring (2/2)\nType-appropriate signal fields are present on every epic:\n`investigation_signals` on E001/E002, `ai_signals` on E003-E006, with no\ncross-wiring. Rationales in the ai-signals files match the frontmatter\nvalues. E002's `cluster_hardware_dependence: -1` is rubric-valid (peripheral\nend-to-end confirmation through