{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-9c2f0b867ca8dc2b","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tstrat_id: RHAISTRAT-8009\n3\tscore: 12\n4\tpass: false\n5\trecommendation: revise\n6\tissues:\n7\t- severity: critical\n8\t  criterion: DAG Coherence\n9\t  description: 'Missing DAG edge discoverable from epic content: E003 scope includes\n10\t    \"If the split-server routing design requires a tracking-server-side configuration\n11\t    change to delegate artifact operations to a dedicated artifacts-only instance,\n12\t    implement that configuration change\" — work contingent on E002 (routing design\n13\t    Investigation) outcome B — yet E003 has no dependency on E002, no gated_by, and\n14\t    no gate_failure_impact for it. Rule 3 requires a blocking edge from an Investigation\n15\t    to all affected Implementations; the summary outcome analysis and E005 gate_failure_impact\n16\t    both state the server-side delegation change lands in the server workstream (E003).\n17\t    Fix by either adding an E002 -> E003 edge with gated_by/gate_failure_impact on\n18\t    E003 (re-deriving priority inheritance, since E002 would then block P0 work) or\n19\t    removing the conditional bullet from E003 scope and modelling the outcome-B server-side\n20\t    change as an explicitly owned and gated work item (e.g., via E005 rewrite or a\n21\t    recorded gate) instead of an ungated scope bullet in an epic with no relation\n22\t    to E002.'\n23\terror: null\n24\t---\n25\t# Decomposition Review: RHAISTRAT-8009\n26\t\n27\t## Review Summary\n28\t\n29\tScore: 12/14 — fail\n30\tRecommendation: revise\n31\t\n32\tContract validator: OK (all mechanical checks passed: epic body sections,\n33\tcanonical component names, Rule 11 docs-last coverage, priority inheritance,\n34\timplementation-type parallelism).\n35\t\n36\tOne critical issue in DAG Coherence (a missing edge discoverable from epic\n37\tcontent) zeroes that criterion; under the auto-fail rule the decomposition\n38\tmust be revised even though every other criterion scores full marks.\n39\t\n40\t## Criterion Details\n41\t\n42\t### 1. HLR Coverage (2/2)\n43\tAll 8 HLRs are mapped. P0 HLR-1 through HLR-4 are delivered by E003 (with\n44\tE004 providing the resolution HLR-2's validation calls); P1 HLR-5/HLR-6 map\n45\tto E005 and HLR-7 to E004. E004's P0 inheritance is correct and documented:\n46\tit blocks E003, which delivers all P0 HLRs. E002 (P1) blocks only E005 (P1),\n47\tand E006 (P1) enables no downstream work, so no lower-priority epic blocks\n48\tP0 work — confirmed by the contract validator. HLR-8 (P2) is bundled into\n49\tE003 with an inline `(bundled)` rationale in the HLR Traceability Matrix row\n50\t(same denial code path as HLR-2/HLR-3; HLR text pins the channel to server\n51\tlogs) — compliant bundling recording, and not priority collapse since the HLR\n52\tshares the implementation surface with the P0 work.\n53\t\n54\t### 2. DAG Coherence (0/2)\n55\tThe mechanically checkable aspects are clean: no circular dependencies; all\n56\tsix diagram edges match the epic frontmatter `dependencies` exactly in both\n57\tdirections; arrows run dependency → dependent; critical path length 4 is\n58\tappropriate for an M-L strategy; E005 ∥ E003/E004 parallelism is correctly\n59\tkept edge-free (Rule 14, different repos, no shared build artifact); no\n60\trepo/konflux/license onboarding epics exist, so Rules 7/8/10 parallelism is\n61\tnot implicated.\n62\t\n63\tHowever, there is a **critical missing edge discoverable from epic\n64\tcontent**: E003's scope contains the bullet \"If the split-server routing\n65\tdesign requires a tracking-server-side configuration change to delegate\n66\tartifact operations to a dedicated artifacts-only instance, implement that\n67\tconfiguration change.\" That bullet consumes E002's routing-design outcome —\n68\tE002 is the epic that determines whether such a server-side change is\n69\trequired. The decomposition summary's investigation-outcome analysis and\n70\tE005's `gate_failure_impact` both confirm that under E002 outcome B \"the\n71\tserver-side delegation config is added to the server workstream's scope\"\n72\t(i.e., E003's scope). Yet E003 has no dependency on E002, no `gated_by`\n73\tE002, and no `gate_failure_impact` covering that outcome. Rule 3 mandates a\n74\tblocking edge from an Investigation to ALL affected Implementations; E003 is\n75\tan affected Implementation by the decomposition's own account. As built,\n76\tE003 (blocked only by E001/E004) can start and complete while E002 (which\n77\truns parallel to E001) is still open, leaving the conditional delegation\n78\twork unowned in the plan if outcome B materializes. The diagram and\n79\tfrontmatter are consistent with each other, but both omit an edge the epic\n80\tcontent requires — per the review contract this is a critical issue and\n81\tzeroes the criterion. Compliant fixes include: adding an E002 → E003 edge\n82\twith `gated_by`/`gate_failure_impact` recorded on E003 (note this would make\n83\tE002 block P0 work, so E002's priority inheritance must be re-derived), or\n84\tremoving the conditional bullet from E003's unconditional scope and\n85\tmodelling the outcome-B server-side change so it is owned and gated\n86\texplicitly (e.g., via E005's rewrite or a recorded gate), rather than as an\n87\tungated scope bullet in an epic with no relation to E002.\n88\t\n89\t### 3. Epic Boundaries (2/2)\n90\tAll epics are MLflow Team with canonical component `MLflow` (the only\n91\tmatching entry in `.context/rhai-components.txt`; the summary documents that\n92\t\"MLflow Operator\" and \"mlflow-kubernetes-plugins\" have no canonical entries\n93\tand would be stripped by submit.py). The E003/E004/E005 split follows\n94\trepo/sub-system seams (server, plugin, operator), not the HLR list. E005's\n95\tsize over the ~2-week guideline is a properly recorded watch item: the\n96\tHealth Warnings entry records the estimate (2-3 sprints), the why-not-split\n97\trationale (one logical operator change, single reconciliation path), and a\n98\tconcrete conditional re-split trigger — shape (b), not docked and no split\n99\tdemanded. No scope duplication: E006 validates the surfaces E003/E005\n100\tproduce rather than rebuilding them. No work-product type mixing: the\n101\t\"document limitations\" item in E003 is an incidental documentation touch-up\n102\texempt from the content-authoring split.\n103\t\n104\t### 4. Type Correctness (2/2)\n105\tBoth Investigations genuinely change downstream scope/existence: E001's\n106\tanswers determine whether E004 is verification or refactor and whether\n107\tE003's path-to-workspace resolution premise holds; E002's design determines\n108\tE005's routing scope. Every `gated_by` target appears in the epic's direct\n109\t`dependencies` (E003→E001, E004→E001, E005→E002), and every `gate_failure_impact`\n110\thas both `action` and `fallback_approach` populated with substantive\n111\trewrites. E006 is correctly ungated (it depends only on Implementations).\n112\tThe ungated E002→E003 relationship is scored under DAG Coherence above.\n113\t\n114\t### 5. AI Implementability Scoring (2/2)\n115\tType-appropriate signal fields are present on every epic:\n116\t`investigation_signals` on E001/E002, `ai_signals` on E003-E006, with no\n117\tcross-wiring. Rationales in the ai-signals files match the frontmatter\n118\tvalues. E002's `cluster_hardware_dependence: -1` is rubric-valid (peripheral\n119\tend-to-end confirmation through Gateway API HTTPRoutes needs a live cluster;\n120\tthe design deliverable itself does not), while E001's 0 is justified by the\n121\tstubbed-Kubernetes-API local exercise. E004/E005's `change_specificity: 0`\n122\tcorrectly reflects surfaces contingent on the gating investigations.\n123\t`architecture_claims: 1` on all four Implementation epics cites\n124\t`.context/architecture-context/architecture/rhoai-3.6-ea.1/mlflow.md` and\n125\t`mlflow-operator.md`, both of which exist and back the cited premises (flag\n126\tset in Dockerfile.konflux CMD, kubernetes-auth plugin, Helm-in-operator\n127\twith Server-Side Apply, conditional HTTPRoute CRD discovery).\n128\t\n129\t### 6. Acceptance Criteria Quality (2/2)\n130\tEvery epic has testable Given/When/Then acceptance criteria with measured-by\n131\tclauses derived from the strategy's own acceptance criteria. No\n132\trule-mandated AC applies (no capability replacement, no konflux chain, no\n133\tdocs-authoring epic); E005 nonetheless carries an opt-in/rollback-safety AC\n134\t(rendered-resource parity with pre-change output). No AC states an\n135\texternally-controlled precondition — the fail-closed, 403, 503, flag, and\n136\trouting criteria all condition on what the epics themselves build or\n137\tverify. No epic body references sibling epics by draft ID; cross-epic\n138\treferences use workstream names.\n139\t\n140\t### 7. Completeness (2/2)\n141\tAll six strategy acceptance criteria are covered: AC 1-4 by E003's ACs (and\n142\tre-validated end to end by E006), AC 5-6 by E005's ACs. All capabilities\n143\tfrom the Technical Approach are scoped (flag composition, workspace\n144\tvalidation with 403, fail-closed 503, backwards compatibility, split-server\n145\ttopology, traffic routing, plugin resolution, audit logging via HLR-8).\n146\tStrategy-level context is carried forward, not dropped: the latency open\n147\tquestion and deferrable caching appear as an E003 risk plus a Health\n148\tWarning; the audit-telemetry open question and pending process gates appear\n149\tas Health Warnings; the S3-scoping risk mitigation is folded into E001 and\n150\tE003 scope. The strategy's Prerequisites section lists Documentation\n151\tSupport as \"Pending human review\", not \"Yes\", so no docs-authoring epic is\n152\trequired (Rule 11), and no substantive content authoring exists in scope.\n153\tAll three Staff Engineer overrides are correctly applied (no new flag, split\n154\tdeployment rather than config toggle, no SSAR mechanics dictated at epic\n155\tlevel).\n156\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-ae698ca7dc970245","agent_id":"a0ba675e7468d39d5","session_id":"994175ee-dc03-4507-860b-d2f574f96031","uuid":"0be83fc8-82d3-43b3-a64e-87b99c70933f","timestamp":"2026-10-26T20:56:25.298Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-9c2f0b867ca8dc2b","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"claude","task_description":"Revise RHAISTRAT-8009 decomposition"}
