{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-8bcccbec5598e44f","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-8009\n3\tepic_count: 6\n4\tcritical_path_length: 4\n5\tdecompose_complete: true\n6\ttriage: null\n7\ttriage_rationale: null\n8\t---\n9\t# Decomposition Summary: RHAISTRAT-8009\n10\t\n11\tWorkspace-aware artifact serving for multi-tenant MLflow deployments.\n12\t\n13\t## Triage\n14\t\n15\tNot triaged. The strategy is M-L (4-7 sprints), spans three affected components\n16\t(MLflow, MLflow Operator, mlflow-kubernetes-plugins) with both server-side and\n17\toperator-topology work streams, and contains open questions and\n18\tvalidation-flagged assumptions that gate downstream scope. Full decomposition\n19\tfollows.\n20\t\n21\t## Staff Engineer Input Overrides\n22\t\n23\tThe Staff Engineer / SME Input section is non-empty and takes precedence. Three\n24\toverrides were applied; the strategy body already reflects all three, and this\n25\tdecomposition follows the corrected strategy:\n26\t\n27\t1. **No new flag — combine existing flags.** The AI-generated technical\n28\t   approach's proposed `--artifacts-only-workspace-aware` flag and\n29\t   `spec.artifactsOnly.workspaceAware` CR field are removed. All epics scope\n30\t   the capability as composition of the existing `--artifacts-only` and\n31\t   `--enable-workspaces` flags with a required explicit `--workspace-store-uri`.\n32\t2. **Operator work is a split deployment, not a config toggle.** The operator\n33\t   epic scopes a dedicated artifacts-only Deployment with its own Service and\n34\t   HTTPRoute plus artifact traffic routing from the main tracking deployment —\n35\t   not a single CR field / Helm values propagation.\n36\t3. **SSAR detail is over-specified.** Workspace authorization reuses the\n37\t   existing auth plugin infrastructure without prescribing Kubernetes API\n38\t   mechanics (no SelfSubjectAccessReview pseudo-resource dictation at epic\n39\t   level). kube-rbac-proxy sidecar and `--app-name kubernetes-auth` are\n40\t   unchanged.\n41\t\n42\t## Component Mapping\n43\t\n44\tAll epics use the canonical Jira component `MLflow` (the only matching entry in\n45\t`.context/rhai-components.txt`). The strategy's \"MLflow Operator\" and\n46\t\"mlflow-kubernetes-plugins\" components have no separate canonical entries:\n47\t`MLflow` is the single most-relevant canonical name for each epic and the\n48\tcomponent span is noted per epic below. `submit.py` would silently strip\n49\tnon-canonical values, so no epic uses \"MLflow Operator\" or\n50\t\"mlflow-kubernetes-plugins\" verbatim.\n51\t\n52\t## Epic List\n53\t\n54\t| ID | Title | Type | Team | Priority |\n55\t|----|-------|------|------|----------|\n56\t| RHAISTRAT-8009-E001 | Workspace resolution feasibility in the artifacts-only server context | Investigation | MLflow Team | P0 |\n57\t| RHAISTRAT-8009-E002 | Artifact traffic routing design for the split-server topology | Investigation | MLflow Team | P1 |\n58\t| RHAISTRAT-8009-E003 | Workspace-aware artifacts-only serving in the MLflow tracking server | Implementation | MLflow Team | P0 |\n59\t| RHAISTRAT-8009-E004 | mlflow-kubernetes-plugins workspace resolution in the artifacts-only context | Implementation | MLflow Team | P0 |\n60\t| RHAISTRAT-8009-E005 | MLflow operator split-server topology for the artifacts-only instance | Implementation | MLflow Team | P1 |\n61\t| RHAISTRAT-8009-E006 | End-to-end integration validation of workspace-aware artifact serving | Implementation | MLflow Team | P1 |\n62\t\n63\tPriority notes: E004 carries a P1 HLR (HLR-7) but blocks E003, which delivers\n64\tall P0 HLRs, so it inherits P0 by priority inheritance. E002, E005, and E006\n65\tare P1: they gate or validate only the P1 topology HLRs (HLR-5, HLR-6), and\n66\tE006 enables no downstream work — the P0 HLRs (HLR-1 through HLR-4) are\n67\tdelivered and acceptance-tested within the server implementation epic itself,\n68\tso no P0 work depends on the topology or validation epics.\n69\t\n70\t## Dependency DAG\n71\t\n72\t```mermaid\n73\tgraph TD\n74\t    E001[RHAISTRAT-8009-E001 Workspace resolution feasibility] --> E004[RHAISTRAT-8009-E004 Plugin workspace resolution]\n75\t    E001 --> E003[RHAISTRAT-8009-E003 Server workspace-aware artifacts-only]\n76\t    E004 --> E003\n77\t    E002[RHAISTRAT-8009-E002 Routing design] --> E005[RHAISTRAT-8009-E005 Operator split-server topology]\n78\t    E003 --> E006[RHAISTRAT-8009-E006 End-to-end integration validation]\n79\t    E005 --> E006\n80\t```\n81\t\n82\t## DAG Justification\n83\t\n84\t| Edge | Rule | Rationale |\n85\t|------|------|-----------|\n86\t| E001 → E004 | Rule 3 (Investigation gates downstream) | The feasibility investigation determines whether the plugin's workspace resolution is coupled to tracking store initialization; if coupled, E004's scope is a refactor (rewrite), not just verification. True gate: `rewrite`. |\n87\t| E001 → E003 | Rule 3 (Investigation gates downstream) | The same investigation determines whether artifact request paths carry enough information to resolve workspace membership — the premise of E003's workspace validation. True gate: `rewrite`. |\n88\t| E004 → E003 | Rule 12 (library → consumer serial) | The server's artifact-request workspace validation builds against the plugin's workspace resolution interface (the `mlflow.workspace_provider` entry point loaded via `WorkspaceStoreRegistry`). The consumer waits for the library surface it calls. |\n89\t| E002 → E005 | Rule 3 (Investigation gates downstream) | The routing design determines the operator's HTTPRoute design and whether the tracking server needs a delegation configuration change; it rewrites E005's routing scope. True gate: `rewrite`. |\n90\t| E003 → E006 | Rule 13 (artifact consumer serial) | End-to-end validation exercises the workspace-aware artifacts-only server behavior that E003 produces; the test suite builds against that implemented capability. |\n91\t| E005 → E006 | Rule 13 (artifact consumer serial) | End-to-end validation exercises the split-server topology (dedicated Deployment, Service, HTTPRoute, artifact traffic routing) that the operator work produces. |\n92\t\n93\tParallelism (no edges, by design):\n94\t\n95\t- E001 ∥ E002 — independent unknowns (server/plugin resolution vs. operator\n96\t  routing design); different questions, different codebases.\n97\t- E005 ∥ E003 / E004 — different repositories with no shared build artifact\n98\t  (Rule 14). The operator's rendered-resource acceptance criteria are\n99\t  verifiable without the new server capability; the coupling between operator\n100\t  topology and server behavior is expressed as E006's end-to-end validation,\n101\t  never as a DAG edge.\n102\t\n103\t### Investigation outcome analysis (bounded outcomes, no branch epics)\n104\t\n105\tBoth investigations have bounded outcomes (≤2 each), but in every outcome the\n106\tsame main-plan epics exist — only their scope changes. Per the branch-epic\n107\tcontract (branch files are for epics whose *existence* depends on an outcome),\n108\tthe outcomes are recorded as `gate_failure_impact` on the gated epics instead\n109\tof conditional branch files:\n110\t\n111\t- **E001 outcome A** (resolution feasible, plugin not coupled): E004 reduces to\n112\t  verification plus tests. **Outcome B** (coupled): E004 additionally factors\n113\t  workspace resolution into an independent initialization path — `rewrite`.\n114\t- **E002 outcome A** (Gateway-level path routing suffices): E005 implements\n115\t  routing purely via HTTPRoute resources. **Outcome B** (tracking server needs\n116\t  a delegation configuration change): the server-side delegation config is\n117\t  added to the server workstream's scope and the operator's routing scope is\n118\t  rewritten accordingly — `rewrite`.\n119\t\n120\t## HLR Traceability Matrix\n121\t\n122\t| HLR | Priority | Epic(s) |\n123\t|-----|----------|---------|\n124\t| HLR-1: `--artifacts-only` composable with `--enable-workspaces` + `--workspace-store-uri` | P0 | E003 |\n125\t| HLR-2: artifact requests validated against workspace membership; non-members denied HTTP 403 | P0 | E003 (with E004 providing the resolution it calls) |\n126\t| HLR-3: fail-closed when workspace store unreachable | P0 | E003 |\n127\t| HLR-4: single-tenant / no-workspace-flag deployments retain current behavior | P0 | E003, E005 (operator topology is opt-in), E006 (regression suite) |\n128\t| HLR-5: operator deploys dedicated artifacts-only Deployment + Service + HTTPRoute | P1 | E005 |\n129\t| HLR-6: main tracking deployment routes artifact traffic to the dedicated instance | P1 | E005 |\n130\t| HLR-7: auth plugin workspace resolution works without tracking store initialization | P1 | E004 |\n131\t| HLR-8: audit logging for workspace access denials via server logs | P2 | E003 (bundled) — emitted from the same artifact-request denial code path as HLR-2/HLR-3; a separate epic would instrument the same handlers twice, and the strategy's own HLR text pins the channel to server logs, so it is not separately schedulable |\n132\t\n133\tAll HLRs are covered; no orphaned requirements. HLR-8 is the only\n134\tlower-priority HLR kept bundled, per the inline rationale above.\n135\t\n136\t## Health Warnings\n137\t\n138\t- **E005 exceeds the ~2-week epic guideline (managed).** E005 is 2-3 sprints\n139\t  by the strategy's effort arithmetic. Kept whole because the split-server\n140\t  topology is one logical operator change — a single reconciliation path\n141\t  rendering one coordinated set of Helm resources (Deployment, Service,\n142\t  HTTPRoute, routing config) — and splitting it would fragment the chart\n143\t  templates and reconciliation logic across artificial boundaries. Re-split\n144\t  trigger: the routing design investigation lands on an approach requiring\n145\t  substantive server-side delegation changes in addition to the operator\n146\t  topology, at which point the epic splits into topology-provisioning and\n147\t  traffic-routing epics along that boundary.\n148\t- **No latency target for per-request workspace validation (open question,\n149\t  PM/Engineering).** The strategy's Performance NFR sets no number, and the\n150\t  open question of whether short-TTL caching of workspace membership decisions\n151\t  is required at launch is unresolved. This does not gate any epic's\n152\t  existence or approach (validation is implemented the same way either way);\n153\t  it is recorded as a risk on E003 with caching as a deferrable follow-up.\n154\t  Human follow-up: PM/Engineering to set the acceptable overhead.\n155\t- **Audit telemetry channel (open question, PM/Security).** HLR-8 commits to\n156\t  server logs; whether denials should additionally emit to OTLP or the\n157\t  Kubernetes audit log is unresolved and out of the committed scope. Human\n158\t  follow-up: PM/Security decision; expansion would be a follow-up strategy.\n159\t- **Strategy-level process gates pending.** Architecture Review, ODH/RHOAI\n160\t  Build Onboarding, Licence Validation, and other prerequisites are all\n161\t  \"Pending human review\" at the strategy level. No epic models these as\n162\t  blocking work; if any gate fails review, affected epic scope may need\n163\t  revision.\n164\t- **Initial implementation scoped to the S3 artifact store.** Per the\n165\t  strategy's risk mitigation, artifact path resolution is scoped to the\n166\t  standard RHOAI S3 configuration (`{experiment_id}/{run_id}/artifacts/`);\n167\t  non-standard artifact roots are documented as a limitation (folded into\n168\t  E003 scope), not supported.\n169\t\n170\t## Tiered Delivery\n171\t\n172\tNot applicable. All dependencies are internal and exist today (workspace\n173\tstore, auth plugin, Gateway API, kube-rbac-proxy); there is no external\n174\tdependency with uncertain timing, so no Tier 1 / Tier 2 split is needed.\n175\tPartial value ordering still exists naturally: the server-side capability\n176\t(P0) is deliverable independently of the operator topology (P1), since\n177\tworkspace-aware artifacts-only serving works for manually deployed instances\n178\tbefore the operator automates the split topology.\n179\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-ae698ca7dc970245","agent_id":"a0ba675e7468d39d5","session_id":"994175ee-dc03-4507-860b-d2f574f96031","uuid":"995ff6e2-cfb5-4022-bb4d-f15094496193","timestamp":"2026-10-26T20:56:30.378Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-8bcccbec5598e44f","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"claude","task_description":"Revise RHAISTRAT-8009 decomposition"}
