{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"chatcmpl-61e34ba3-eee3-4a9e-96ec-b9771599fa97","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"chatcmpl-tool-83fedf54ca291ab6","name":"Edit","input":{"replace_all":false,"file_path":"/private/var/folders/h5/l8qslbp55w5621qf9ngsh5jc0000gn/T/epic-RHAISTRAT-8009-ito67jrk/artifacts/epic-tasks/RHAISTRAT-8009-BRANCH-B-E007.md","old_string":"jira_key: null\n---\n","new_string":"jira_key: null\n---\n# Tracking server artifact-delegation configuration for the split-server topology\n\n## Description\n\nConditional work item, selected only when the artifact traffic routing\ndesign investigation determines that Gateway API HTTPRoute-level path\nrouting does not suffice and the tracking server itself needs a\nconfiguration change to delegate artifact operations to the dedicated\nartifacts-only instance. In that outcome, this epic implements the\nserver-side delegation configuration in the MLflow tracking server so\nthat artifact traffic from the main tracking deployment is delegated to\nthe dedicated artifacts-only instance, where workspace validation is\nenforced. If the investigation determines HTTPRoute-level routing\nsuffices, this work item does not exist.\n\n## Scope\n\n- Implement the tracking-server-side delegation configuration specified\n  by the routing design investigation's outcome: the configuration by\n  which the main tracking server delegates artifact operations to the\n  dedicated artifacts-only instance (exact mechanism per the adopted\n  design).\n- Ensure artifact requests delegated to the artifacts-only instance are\n  served by that instance and workspace-validated there — the delegated\n  path must not bypass workspace validation.\n- Backwards compatibility: deployments without the delegation\n  configuration retain current artifact-serving behavior; the\n  configuration is opt-in.\n- Keep the delegation configuration consistent with the existing flag\n  composition (`--artifacts-only` combined with `--enable-workspaces` and\n  an explicit `--workspace-store-uri`); no new server flag is\n  introduced.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- Given the main tracking deployment configured with the delegation\n  configuration per the adopted routing design, when a user requests an\n  artifact through the main tracking deployment's entry point, then the\n  request is delegated to the dedicated artifacts-only instance and\n  served there — measured by the artifacts-only instance's request logs\n  / tracing showing the served request.\n- Given the same deployment and a user who is not a member of the\n  artifact's workspace, when the request is delegated, then it is denied\n  by workspace validation — measured by HTTP 403 from the artifacts-only\n  instance (the delegated path enforces workspace validation).\n- Given a deployment without the delegation configuration, when any\n  authenticated user requests an artifact, then current behavior is\n  retained — measured by artifact-serving behavior parity with the\n  pre-change deployment (opt-in safety).\n- The delegation mechanism matches the specification produced by the\n  routing design investigation — measured by review against that\n  documented design.\n\n## HLR Traceability\n\n- HLR-6 (P1, server-side half): main tracking deployment routes artifact\n  traffic to the dedicated artifacts-only instance — the server-side\n  delegation configuration this epic implements when the routing design\n  selects that mechanism; the operator-side routing configuration is\n  delivered by the operator topology workstream.\n- HLR-4 (P0, backwards-compatibility aspect): the delegation\n  configuration is opt-in; deployments without it retain current\n  behavior.\n\n## Risks / Notes\n\n- This epic exists only under the routing design investigation's outcome\n  requiring a server-side delegation change; it is a conditional branch\n  work item attached to that investigation, not a standalone main-plan\n  epic. It builds on the workspace-aware artifacts-only serving\n  capability and the operator's split-server topology and is sequenced\n  with them in that outcome's plan; end-to-end validation of the\n  delegated routing path is covered by the integration validation\n  workstream's routing scenarios.\n- The exact delegation mechanism is deliberately unspecified here: the\n  routing design investigation's outcome specification is the source of\n  truth for the mechanism, per the strategy's open-question ownership.\n"}}],"model":"rits/zai-org/glm-5-3","stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":55978,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-ae698ca7dc970245","agent_id":"a0ba675e7468d39d5","session_id":"994175ee-dc03-4507-860b-d2f574f96031","uuid":"95c66215-e8ca-4bea-9e8e-15c5e0e3dce6","timestamp":"2026-10-26T21:01:56.719Z","subagent_type":"claude","task_description":"Revise RHAISTRAT-8009 decomposition"}
