{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-8f3d2778cfe903d4","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-8009\n3\tepic_count: 6\n4\tcritical_path_length: 4\n5\tdecompose_complete: true\n6\ttriage: null\n7\ttriage_rationale: null\n8\trevised: true\n9\t---\n10\t# Decomposition Summary: RHAISTRAT-8009\n11\t\n12\tWorkspace-aware artifact serving for multi-tenant MLflow deployments.\n13\t\n14\t## Triage\n15\t\n16\tNot triaged. The strategy is M-L (4-7 sprints), spans three affected components\n17\t(MLflow, MLflow Operator, mlflow-kubernetes-plugins) with both server-side and\n18\toperator-topology work streams, and contains open questions and\n19\tvalidation-flagged assumptions that gate downstream scope. Full decomposition\n20\tfollows.\n21\t\n22\t## Staff Engineer Input Overrides\n23\t\n24\tThe Staff Engineer / SME Input section is non-empty and takes precedence. Three\n25\toverrides were applied; the strategy body already reflects all three, and this\n26\tdecomposition follows the corrected strategy:\n27\t\n28\t1. **No new flag — combine existing flags.** The AI-generated technical\n29\t   approach's proposed `--artifacts-only-workspace-aware` flag and\n30\t   `spec.artifactsOnly.workspaceAware` CR field are removed. All epics scope\n31\t   the capability as composition of the existing `--artifacts-only` and\n32\t   `--enable-workspaces` flags with a required explicit `--workspace-store-uri`.\n33\t2. **Operator work is a split deployment, not a config toggle.** The operator\n34\t   epic scopes a dedicated artifacts-only Deployment with its own Service and\n35\t   HTTPRoute plus artifact traffic routing from the main tracking deployment —\n36\t   not a single CR field / Helm values propagation.\n37\t3. **SSAR detail is over-specified.** Workspace authorization reuses the\n38\t   existing auth plugin infrastructure without prescribing Kubernetes API\n39\t   mechanics (no SelfSubjectAccessReview pseudo-resource dictation at epic\n40\t   level). kube-rbac-proxy sidecar and `--app-name kubernetes-auth` are\n41\t   unchanged.\n42\t\n43\t## Component Mapping\n44\t\n45\tAll epics use the canonical Jira component `MLflow` (the only matching entry in\n46\t`.context/rhai-components.txt`). The strategy's \"MLflow Operator\" and\n47\t\"mlflow-kubernetes-plugins\" components have no separate canonical entries:\n48\t`MLflow` is the single most-relevant canonical name for each epic and the\n49\tcomponent span is noted per epic below. `submit.py` would silently strip\n50\tnon-canonical values, so no epic uses \"MLflow Operator\" or\n51\t\"mlflow-kubernetes-plugins\" verbatim.\n52\t\n53\t## Epic List\n54\t\n55\t| ID | Title | Type | Team | Priority |\n56\t|----|-------|------|------|----------|\n57\t| RHAISTRAT-8009-E001 | Workspace resolution feasibility in the artifacts-only server context | Investigation | MLflow Team | P0 |\n58\t| RHAISTRAT-8009-E002 | Artifact traffic routing design for the split-server topology | Investigation | MLflow Team | P1 |\n59\t| RHAISTRAT-8009-E003 | Workspace-aware artifacts-only serving in the MLflow tracking server | Implementation | MLflow Team | P0 |\n60\t| RHAISTRAT-8009-E004 | mlflow-kubernetes-plugins workspace resolution in the artifacts-only context | Implementation | MLflow Team | P0 |\n61\t| RHAISTRAT-8009-E005 | MLflow operator split-server topology for the artifacts-only instance | Implementation | MLflow Team | P1 |\n62\t| RHAISTRAT-8009-E006 | End-to-end integration validation of workspace-aware artifact serving | Implementation | MLflow Team | P1 |\n63\t\n64\tOne conditional branch epic exists outside the main plan:\n65\tRHAISTRAT-8009-BRANCH-B-E007 (Tracking server artifact-delegation\n66\tconfiguration for the split-server topology, Implementation, MLflow Team,\n67\tP1, component MLflow), selected only by the routing design investigation's\n68\toutcome B — see \"Conditional branch epic\" below. It is excluded from\n69\t`epic_count` and the main-plan critical path.\n70\t\n71\tPriority notes: E004 carries a P1 HLR (HLR-7) but blocks E003, which delivers\n72\tall P0 HLRs, so it inherits P0 by priority inheritance. E002, E005, and E006\n73\tare P1: they gate or validate only the P1 topology HLRs (HLR-5, HLR-6), and\n74\tE006 enables no downstream work — the P0 HLRs (HLR-1 through HLR-4) are\n75\tdelivered and acceptance-tested within the server implementation epic itself,\n76\tso no P0 work depends on the topology or validation epics.\n77\t\n78\t## Dependency DAG\n79\t\n80\t```mermaid\n81\tgraph TD\n82\t    E001[RHAISTRAT-8009-E001 Workspace resolution feasibility] --> E004[RHAISTRAT-8009-E004 Plugin workspace resolution]\n83\t    E001 --> E003[RHAISTRAT-8009-E003 Server workspace-aware artifacts-only]\n84\t    E004 --> E003\n85\t    E002[RHAISTRAT-8009-E002 Routing design] --> E005[RHAISTRAT-8009-E005 Operator split-server topology]\n86\t    E003 --> E006[RHAISTRAT-8009-E006 End-to-end integration validation]\n87\t    E005 --> E006\n88\t```\n89\t\n90\t## DAG Justification\n91\t\n92\t| Edge | Rule | Rationale |\n93\t|------|------|-----------|\n94\t| E001 → E004 | Rule 3 (Investigation gates downstream) | The feasibility investigation determines whether the plugin's workspace resolution is coupled to tracking store initialization; if coupled, E004's scope is a refactor (rewrite), not just verification. True gate: `rewrite`. |\n95\t| E001 → E003 | Rule 3 (Investigation gates downstream) | The same investigation determines whether artifact request paths carry enough information to resolve workspace membership — the premise of E003's workspace validation. True gate: `rewrite`. |\n96\t| E004 → E003 | Rule 12 (library → consumer serial) | The server's artifact-request workspace validation builds against the plugin's workspace resolution interface (the `mlflow.workspace_provider` entry point loaded via `WorkspaceStoreRegistry`). The consumer waits for the library surface it calls. |\n97\t| E002 → E005 | Rule 3 (Investigation gates downstream) | The routing design determines the operator's HTTPRoute design and whether the tracking server needs a delegation configuration change; it rewrites E005's routing scope. True gate: `rewrite`. |\n98\t| E003 → E006 | Rule 13 (artifact consumer serial) | End-to-end validation exercises the workspace-aware artifacts-only server behavior that E003 produces; the test suite builds against that implemented capability. |\n99\t| E005 → E006 | Rule 13 (artifact consumer serial) | End-to-end validation exercises the split-server topology (dedicated Deployment, Service, HTTPRoute, artifact traffic routing) that the operator work produces. |\n100\t\n101\tParallelism (no edges, by design):\n102\t\n103\t- E001 ∥ E002 — independent unknowns (server/plugin resolution vs. operator\n104\t  routing design); different questions, different codebases.\n105\t- E005 ∥ E003 / E004 — different repositories with no shared build artifact\n106\t  (Rule 14). The operator's rendered-resource acceptance criteria are\n107\t  verifiable without the new server capability; the coupling between operator\n108\t  topology and server behavior is expressed as E006's end-to-end validation,\n109\t  never as a DAG edge.\n110\t\n111\t### Investigation outcome analysis (bounded outcomes)\n112\t\n113\tBoth investigations have bounded outcomes (≤2 each). E001's outcomes change\n114\tonly the scope of existing main-plan epics, so they are recorded as\n115\t`gate_failure_impact` on the gated epics. E002's outcome B, however, adds a\n116\twork item that does not exist in outcome A — a tracking-server-side\n117\tdelegation configuration — so per the branch-epic contract (branch files\n118\tare for epics whose *existence* depends on an outcome) it is modelled as a\n119\tconditional branch epic rather than as unconditional scope inside a\n120\tmain-plan epic:\n121\t\n122\t- **E001 outcome A** (resolution feasible, plugin not coupled): E004 reduces to\n123\t  verification plus tests. **Outcome B** (coupled): E004 additionally factors\n124\t  workspace resolution into an independent initialization path — `rewrite`.\n125\t- **E002 outcome A** (Gateway-level path routing suffices): E005 implements\n126\t  routing purely via HTTPRoute resources; no server-side change exists.\n127\t  **Outcome B** (tracking server needs a delegation configuration change):\n128\t  the operator's routing scope is rewritten accordingly — `rewrite` — and the\n129\t  server-side delegation configuration is owned by the conditional branch\n130\t  epic **RHAISTRAT-8009-BRANCH-B-E007** (gated on this outcome; `remove` if\n131\t  outcome A holds). The main-plan server epic (workspace-aware\n132\t  artifacts-only serving) is deliberately not blocked on the routing design:\n133\t  its P0 scope (flag composition, workspace validation, fail-closed,\n134\t  backwards compatibility) is independent of the topology decision, and the\n135\t  delegation configuration serves only the P1 topology HLRs — so gating it\n136\t  on the routing investigation would serialize P0 delivery behind P1\n137\t  topology work and force the routing investigation to inherit P0.\n138\t\n139\t### Conditional branch epic (E002 outcome B)\n140\t\n141\tOne branch file exists, selected only when the routing design investigation\n142\tdetermines the tracking server needs a configuration change to delegate\n143\tartifact operations:\n144\t\n145\t- **RHAISTRAT-8009-BRANCH-B-E007** — Tracking server artifact-delegation\n146\t  configuration for the split-server topology (Implementation, MLflow Team,\n147\t  P1, component MLflow). Gated by the routing design investigation\n148\t  (`gated_by: RHAISTRAT-8009-E002`, `branch: B`,\n149\t  `gate_failure_impact.action: remove`): under outcome A (HTTPRoute-level\n150\t  routing suffices) the work item does not exist.\n151\t\n152\t```mermaid\n153\tgraph TD\n154\t    E002[RHAISTRAT-8009-E002 Routing design] --> B007[RHAISTRAT-8009-BRANCH-B-E007 Server delegation config]\n155\t```\n156\t\n157\tBranch epics are excluded from the main-plan DAG and critical path above,\n158\tand a main-plan epic never lists one in its dependencies. `epic_count`\n159\tcounts the six main-plan epics.\n160\t\n161\t## HLR Traceability Matrix\n162\t\n163\t| HLR | Priority | Epic(s) |\n164\t|-----|----------|---------|\n165\t| HLR-1: `--artifacts-only` composable with `--enable-workspaces` + `--workspace-store-uri` | P0 | E003 |\n166\t| HLR-2: artifact requests validated against workspace membership; non-members denied HTTP 403 | P0 | E003 (with E004 providing the resolution it calls) |\n167\t| HLR-3: fail-closed when workspace store unreachable | P0 | E003 |\n168\t| HLR-4: single-tenant / no-workspace-flag deployments retain current behavior | P0 | E003, E005 (operator topology is opt-in), E006 (regression suite) |\n169\t| HLR-5: operator deploys dedicated artifacts-only Deployment + Service + HTTPRoute | P1 | E005 |\n170\t| HLR-6: main tracking deployment routes artifact traffic to the dedicated instance | P1 | E005 (operator-side routing), and RHAISTRAT-8009-BRANCH-B-E007 for the server-side delegation half if the routing design requires it |\n171\t| HLR-7: auth plugin workspace resolution works without tracking store initialization | P1 | E004 |\n172\t| HLR-8: audit logging for workspace access denials via server logs | P2 | E003 (bundled) — emitted from the same artifact-request denial code path as HLR-2/HLR-3; a separate epic would instrument the same handlers twice, and the strategy's own HLR text pins the channel to server logs, so it is not separately schedulable |\n173\t\n174\tAll HLRs are covered; no orphaned requirements. HLR-8 is the only\n175\tlower-priority HLR kept bundled, per the inline rationale above.\n176\t\n177\t## Health Warnings\n178\t\n179\t- **E005 exceeds the ~2-week epic guideline (managed).** E005 is 2-3 sprints\n180\t  by the strategy's effort arithmetic. Kept whole because the split-server\n181\t  topology is one logical operator change — a single reconciliation path\n182\t  rendering one coordinated set of Helm resources (Deployment, Service,\n183\t  HTTPRoute, routing config) — and splitting it would fragment the chart\n184\t  templates and reconciliation logic across artificial boundaries. Re-split\n185\t  trigger: the routing design investigation lands on an approach requiring\n186\t  substantive server-side delegation changes in addition to the operator\n187\t  topology, at which point the epic splits into topology-provisioning and\n188\t  traffic-routing epics along that boundary.\n189\t- **No latency target for per-request workspace validation (open question,\n190\t  PM/Engineering).** The strategy's Performance NFR sets no number, and the\n191\t  open question of whether short-TTL caching of workspace membership decisions\n192\t  is required at launch is unresolved. This does not gate any epic's\n193\t  existence or approach (validation is implemented the same way either way);\n194\t  it is recorded as a risk on E003 with caching as a deferrable follow-up.\n195\t  Human follow-up: PM/Engineering to set the acceptable overhead.\n196\t- **Audit telemetry channel (open question, PM/Security).** HLR-8 commits to\n197\t  server logs; whether denials should additionally emit to OTLP or the\n198\t  Kubernetes audit log is unresolved and out of the committed scope. Human\n199\t  follow-up: PM/Security decision; expansion would be a follow-up strategy.\n200\t- **Strategy-level process gates pending.** Architecture Review, ODH/RHOAI\n201\t  Build Onboarding, Licence Validation, and other prerequisites are all\n202\t  \"Pending human review\" at the strategy level. No epic models these as\n203\t  blocking work; if any gate fails review, affected epic scope may need\n204\t  revision.\n205\t- **Initial implementation scoped to the S3 artifact store.** Per the\n206\t  strategy's risk mitigation, artifact path resolution is scoped to the\n207\t  standard RHOAI S3 configuration (`{experiment_id}/{run_id}/artifacts/`);\n208\t  non-standard artifact roots are documented as a limitation (folded into\n209\t  E003 scope), not supported.\n210\t\n211\t## Tiered Delivery\n212\t\n213\tNot applicable. All dependencies are internal and exist today (workspace\n214\tstore, auth plugin, Gateway API, kube-rbac-proxy); there is no external\n215\tdependency with uncertain timing, so no Tier 1 / Tier 2 split is needed.\n216\tPartial value ordering still exists naturally: the server-side capability\n217\t(P0) is deliverable independently of the operator topology (P1), since\n218\tworkspace-aware artifacts-only serving works for manually deployed instances\n219\tbefore the operator automates the split topology.\n220\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-b4ec5560b1a4b7b6","agent_id":"abc26509b17a1d42b","session_id":"994175ee-dc03-4507-860b-d2f574f96031","uuid":"3ff5b9c1-3038-47f5-af2f-1fcd27bec89a","timestamp":"2026-10-26T21:03:51.352Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-8f3d2778cfe903d4","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"claude","task_description":"Re-review RHAISTRAT-8009 decomposition"}
