{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-baa6bd7ec8d4262e","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tepic_id: RHAISTRAT-8009-E003\n3\ttitle: Workspace-aware artifacts-only serving in the MLflow tracking server\n4\tparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-8009\n5\tcomponent: MLflow\n6\tteam: MLflow Team\n7\ttype: Implementation\n8\tpriority: P0\n9\tdependencies:\n10\t- RHAISTRAT-8009-E001\n11\t- RHAISTRAT-8009-E004\n12\tgated_by: RHAISTRAT-8009-E001\n13\tgate_failure_impact:\n14\t  action: rewrite\n15\t  fallback_approach: If artifact request paths cannot resolve to workspace membership\n16\t    or the workspace provider cannot be initialized without tracking stores, the workspace\n17\t    validation approach in the server is redesigned per the investigation's findings\n18\t    (e.g., alternative path-to-workspace resolution or a changed initialization sequence)\n19\t    before this epic's implementation approach is final.\n20\tai_signals:\n21\t  change_specificity: 1\n22\t  pattern_precedent: 1\n23\t  adapter_pattern: 1\n24\t  existing_foundation: 1\n25\t  open_questions: 0\n26\t  external_dependency: 1\n27\t  human_process_gates: 0\n28\t  repo_access: 1\n29\t  architecture_claims: 1\n30\timplementation_type: null\n31\tinvestigation_signals: null\n32\tai_implementability: High\n33\tai_implementability_score: 7\n34\tjira_key: null\n35\tbranch: null\n36\t---\n37\t# Workspace-aware artifacts-only serving in the MLflow tracking server\n38\t\n39\t## Description\n40\t\n41\tMake the MLflow tracking server's `--artifacts-only` mode composable with the\n42\texisting `--enable-workspaces` flag so that the artifacts-only server\n43\tvalidates artifact requests against workspace (tenant) boundaries. When both\n44\tflags are set, the server requires an explicit `--workspace-store-uri`,\n45\tinitializes only the workspace provider needed to validate artifact requests,\n46\tkeeps tracking and model registry stores uninitialized, and keeps\n47\tnon-artifact API endpoints disabled. No new server flag is introduced — the\n48\tworkspace-aware behavior emerges from combining existing flags.\n49\t\n50\t## Scope\n51\t\n52\t- Server flag composition: allow `--artifacts-only` to combine with\n53\t  `--enable-workspaces`; when both are set, require an explicit\n54\t  `--workspace-store-uri` (server fails to start with a clear error if it is\n55\t  missing) and initialize only the workspace provider.\n56\t- Keep tracking stores and model registry stores uninitialized and\n57\t  non-artifact API endpoints disabled in the combined mode.\n58\t- Workspace validation in artifact request handling: for each artifact\n59\t  request, resolve the artifact path to its parent experiment (artifacts are\n60\t  stored under experiment-scoped paths, e.g.\n61\t  `{experiment_id}/{run_id}/artifacts/`), look up the experiment's workspace\n62\t  assignment via the workspace store, and delegate authorization to the\n63\t  existing auth plugin infrastructure. Requests failing workspace validation\n64\t  receive HTTP 403.\n65\t- Fail-closed behavior: when workspace-aware mode is active but the workspace\n66\t  store is unreachable, artifact requests are denied (HTTP 503 indicating\n67\t  workspace validation is unavailable), never served without validation.\n68\t- Backwards compatibility: `--artifacts-only` without `--enable-workspaces`\n69\t  retains current behavior (no workspace validation); deployments that do not\n70\t  set `--enable-workspaces` are unaffected; no existing API contracts, CLI\n71\t  arguments, or configuration keys change meaning.\n72\t- Audit logging of workspace access denials in artifact serving through\n73\t  server logs (denials emit a log record on the same denial code path).\n74\t- Document limitations of artifact path resolution for non-standard artifact\n75\t  roots (initial implementation is scoped to the S3 artifact store, the\n76\t  standard RHOAI configuration).\n77\t\n78\t## Acceptance Criteria\n79\t\n80\t- Given the server started with `--artifacts-only --enable-workspaces\n81\t  --workspace-store-uri kubernetes://` and a user who is a member of\n82\t  workspace A, when the user requests an artifact belonging to an experiment\n83\t  in workspace A, then the artifact is served — measured by HTTP 200 with\n84\t  correct artifact content.\n85\t- Given the same deployment and a user who is a member of workspace A but not\n86\t  workspace B, when the user requests an artifact belonging to an experiment\n87\t  in workspace B, then the request is denied — measured by HTTP 403 with an\n88\t  error message indicating insufficient workspace permissions, and a\n89\t  corresponding audit log record in the server logs.\n90\t- Given the server started with `--artifacts-only` without\n91\t  `--enable-workspaces`, when any authenticated user requests an artifact,\n92\t  then it is served without workspace validation — measured by HTTP 200\n93\t  regardless of workspace membership (current behavior preserved).\n94\t- Given the workspace-aware artifacts-only server running and the workspace\n95\t  store (Kubernetes API) unreachable, when a user requests an artifact, then\n96\t  the request is denied — measured by HTTP 503 indicating workspace\n97\t  validation is unavailable (fail-closed).\n98\t- Given `--artifacts-only --enable-workspaces` set without\n99\t  `--workspace-store-uri`, the server fails to start with a clear error\n100\t  message.\n101\t- In the combined mode, tracking and model registry stores remain\n102\t  uninitialized and non-artifact API endpoints remain disabled — measured by\n103\t  server startup behavior and endpoint availability.\n104\t- Regression: server behavior with either flag used individually (and with\n105\t  neither) is unchanged from current behavior — measured by the existing\n106\t  server test suite passing.\n107\t- Limitations of artifact path resolution for non-standard artifact roots are\n108\t  documented.\n109\t\n110\t## HLR Traceability\n111\t\n112\t- HLR-1 (P0): `--artifacts-only` composable with `--enable-workspaces` and\n113\t  `--workspace-store-uri` for workspace-validated artifact serving.\n114\t- HLR-2 (P0): artifact requests validated against workspace membership;\n115\t  non-members denied HTTP 403.\n116\t- HLR-3 (P0): fail-closed when the workspace store is unreachable.\n117\t- HLR-4 (P0): single-tenant / no-workspace-flag deployments retain current\n118\t  behavior.\n119\t- HLR-8 (P2, bundled): audit logging for workspace access denials through\n120\t  server logs — emitted from the same artifact-request denial code path as\n121\t  HLR-2/HLR-3; instrumented here rather than in a separate epic because a\n122\t  split would touch the same handlers twice.\n123\t\n124\t## Risks / Notes\n125\t\n126\t- Artifact path resolution complexity: artifact paths may not map\n127\t  straightforwardly to experiment IDs in all storage backends or custom\n128\t  artifact root configurations. Mitigation: scope to the S3 artifact store\n129\t  (standard RHOAI configuration) and document limitations for non-standard\n130\t  artifact roots.\n131\t- Performance: workspace resolution adds a workspace store lookup and auth\n132\t  plugin validation per artifact request. No latency target is specified;\n133\t  short-TTL caching of workspace membership decisions (consistent with\n134\t  existing caching patterns in the tracking server's auth path) is a\n135\t  deferrable follow-up pending the PM/Engineering latency decision.\n136\t- Workspace validation delegates to the plugin's workspace resolution, which\n137\t  must function in the artifacts-only context — the plugin workstream\n138\t  provides that capability.\n139\t- Scope boundary — split-server delegation configuration: the artifact\n140\t  traffic routing design investigation may determine that the tracking\n141\t  server needs a configuration change to delegate artifact operations to a\n142\t  dedicated artifacts-only instance. That change is contingent on the\n143\t  routing design's outcome and serves the split-server topology (P1\n144\t  HLRs), not this epic's workspace-aware artifacts-only capability (P0\n145\t  HLRs, which are deliverable without the topology). It is therefore\n146\t  scoped and gated as a conditional work item under that investigation's\n147\t  outcome rather than carried as unconditional scope in this epic, so\n148\t  that P0 server delivery is not blocked by the topology routing\n149\t  decision.\n150\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-b4ec5560b1a4b7b6","agent_id":"abc26509b17a1d42b","session_id":"994175ee-dc03-4507-860b-d2f574f96031","uuid":"6f60d034-a89a-46f1-bad5-82910f6d3724","timestamp":"2026-10-26T21:03:55.897Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-baa6bd7ec8d4262e","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"claude","task_description":"Re-review RHAISTRAT-8009 decomposition"}
