{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-99002e5f57c74c9d","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tepic_id: RHAISTRAT-8009-BRANCH-B-E007\n3\ttitle: Tracking server artifact-delegation configuration for the split-server topology\n4\tparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-8009\n5\tcomponent: MLflow\n6\tteam: MLflow Team\n7\ttype: Implementation\n8\tpriority: P1\n9\tdependencies:\n10\t- RHAISTRAT-8009-E002\n11\tgated_by: RHAISTRAT-8009-E002\n12\tbranch: B\n13\tgate_failure_impact:\n14\t  action: remove\n15\t  fallback_approach: If the routing design investigation determines that Gateway API\n16\t    HTTPRoute-level path routing suffices without a tracking-server-side change, this\n17\t    work item does not exist — artifact traffic routing is implemented purely via\n18\t    Gateway API resources in the operator workstream.\n19\tai_signals:\n20\t  change_specificity: 0\n21\t  pattern_precedent: 1\n22\t  adapter_pattern: 1\n23\t  existing_foundation: 1\n24\t  open_questions: 0\n25\t  external_dependency: 1\n26\t  human_process_gates: 0\n27\t  repo_access: 1\n28\t  architecture_claims: 1\n29\tai_implementability: High\n30\tai_implementability_score: 6\n31\timplementation_type: null\n32\tinvestigation_signals: null\n33\tjira_key: null\n34\t---\n35\t# Tracking server artifact-delegation configuration for the split-server topology\n36\t\n37\t## Description\n38\t\n39\tConditional work item, selected only when the artifact traffic routing\n40\tdesign investigation determines that Gateway API HTTPRoute-level path\n41\trouting does not suffice and the tracking server itself needs a\n42\tconfiguration change to delegate artifact operations to the dedicated\n43\tartifacts-only instance. In that outcome, this epic implements the\n44\tserver-side delegation configuration in the MLflow tracking server so\n45\tthat artifact traffic from the main tracking deployment is delegated to\n46\tthe dedicated artifacts-only instance, where workspace validation is\n47\tenforced. If the investigation determines HTTPRoute-level routing\n48\tsuffices, this work item does not exist.\n49\t\n50\t## Scope\n51\t\n52\t- Implement the tracking-server-side delegation configuration specified\n53\t  by the routing design investigation's outcome: the configuration by\n54\t  which the main tracking server delegates artifact operations to the\n55\t  dedicated artifacts-only instance (exact mechanism per the adopted\n56\t  design).\n57\t- Ensure artifact requests delegated to the artifacts-only instance are\n58\t  served by that instance and workspace-validated there — the delegated\n59\t  path must not bypass workspace validation.\n60\t- Backwards compatibility: deployments without the delegation\n61\t  configuration retain current artifact-serving behavior; the\n62\t  configuration is opt-in.\n63\t- Keep the delegation configuration consistent with the existing flag\n64\t  composition (`--artifacts-only` combined with `--enable-workspaces` and\n65\t  an explicit `--workspace-store-uri`); no new server flag is\n66\t  introduced.\n67\t\n68\t## Acceptance Criteria\n69\t\n70\t- Given the main tracking deployment configured with the delegation\n71\t  configuration per the adopted routing design, when a user requests an\n72\t  artifact through the main tracking deployment's entry point, then the\n73\t  request is delegated to the dedicated artifacts-only instance and\n74\t  served there — measured by the artifacts-only instance's request logs\n75\t  / tracing showing the served request.\n76\t- Given the same deployment and a user who is not a member of the\n77\t  artifact's workspace, when the request is delegated, then it is denied\n78\t  by workspace validation — measured by HTTP 403 from the artifacts-only\n79\t  instance (the delegated path enforces workspace validation).\n80\t- Given a deployment without the delegation configuration, when any\n81\t  authenticated user requests an artifact, then current behavior is\n82\t  retained — measured by artifact-serving behavior parity with the\n83\t  pre-change deployment (opt-in safety).\n84\t- The delegation mechanism matches the specification produced by the\n85\t  routing design investigation — measured by review against that\n86\t  documented design.\n87\t\n88\t## HLR Traceability\n89\t\n90\t- HLR-6 (P1, server-side half): main tracking deployment routes artifact\n91\t  traffic to the dedicated artifacts-only instance — the server-side\n92\t  delegation configuration this epic implements when the routing design\n93\t  selects that mechanism; the operator-side routing configuration is\n94\t  delivered by the operator topology workstream.\n95\t- HLR-4 (P0, backwards-compatibility aspect): the delegation\n96\t  configuration is opt-in; deployments without it retain current\n97\t  behavior.\n98\t\n99\t## Risks / Notes\n100\t\n101\t- This epic exists only under the routing design investigation's outcome\n102\t  requiring a server-side delegation change; it is a conditional branch\n103\t  work item attached to that investigation, not a standalone main-plan\n104\t  epic. It builds on the workspace-aware artifacts-only serving\n105\t  capability and the operator's split-server topology and is sequenced\n106\t  with them in that outcome's plan; end-to-end validation of the\n107\t  delegated routing path is covered by the integration validation\n108\t  workstream's routing scenarios.\n109\t- The exact delegation mechanism is deliberately unspecified here: the\n110\t  routing design investigation's outcome specification is the source of\n111\t  truth for the mechanism, per the strategy's open-question ownership.\n112\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-b4ec5560b1a4b7b6","agent_id":"abc26509b17a1d42b","session_id":"994175ee-dc03-4507-860b-d2f574f96031","uuid":"54bcb11a-9d20-487d-aace-31a2eac40430","timestamp":"2026-10-26T21:03:58.481Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-99002e5f57c74c9d","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_typ